  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Directly transferable nucleoside-analog contexts show measured oral F above 20%: zalcitabine ~80% in fasted patients (C29), didanosine fasting F 0.22–0.28 (C08, C14), zidovudine F 0.61±0.22 in cirrhosis (C10), didanosine 42% (C45), children F 0.27±0.13 (C68), and zalcitabine 30% in fasted mice (C62).",
      "card_ids": ["C29", "C08", "C14", "C10", "C45", "C68", "C62"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The close polar ribonucleoside ribavirin is highly soluble across physiological pH (C141, C133, C138) and shows substantial oral exposure (AUC0-12 3325.83 ng·h/mL, Tmax 1.61 h; C92, C82); lamivudine 300 mg also shows substantial oral exposure (AUC0-inf 13.2 µg·h/mL, Cmax 3.3 µg/mL; C100, C119). This supports that polar ribonucleosides can achieve F≥20% despite low passive permeability.",
      "card_ids": ["C141", "C133", "C138", "C92", "C82", "C100", "C119"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 provides mechanistic support for transporter-mediated intestinal uptake of polar nucleosides: NHC is a substrate of CNT1/2/3/ENT2 (C216), gemcitabine entry is transporter-dependent (C205), lamivudine has measurable Caco-2 uptake permeability (C197), didanosine shows L→B intestinal perfusion 1.4×10^-6 cm/s (C200), F-ddI shows L→B 3.3×10^-6 cm/s (C191), and a nucleoside sulfamate showed no efflux (C231).",
      "card_ids": ["C216", "C205", "C197", "C200", "C191", "C231"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 shows that nucleoside analogs can have low hepatic metabolic clearance: GS-441524 is not a substrate of major CYP isoforms (C264, C276), zidovudine has a low predicted hepatic clearance of 1.7 ml/min/kg from HLM scaling (C236, C274), and EdUrd has a low hepatic clearance of 1.29 ml/min in isolated perfused rat liver (C279). This supports that hepatic first-pass is not an automatic barrier for this class.",
      "card_ids": ["C264", "C276", "C236", "C274", "C279"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The only sub-20% nucleoside F record, gemcitabine in mice (C47), is low-transferability and reflects a different metabolic liability (deamination); low passive permeability and efflux for other nucleosides (C132, C178, C172) are mechanism-only and do not affirmatively establish F<20% for AICAR.",
      "card_ids": ["C47", "C132", "C178", "C172"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C264", "C276", "C236", "C274", "C279"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct absolute oral bioavailability measurement for AICAR/acadesine is present in the supplied records.",
    "No AICAR-specific hepatic clearance or metabolic stability data are present; L6 transfer is from other nucleosides such as GS-441524, zidovudine, and EdUrd.",
    "No AICAR-specific permeability, transporter, efflux, or intestinal metabolism experiment is present; L4/L5 transfer is from other nucleosides such as ribavirin, lamivudine, NHC, didanosine, and gemcitabine.",
    "Analog oral F values are variable (e.g., didanosine 22–42%, zalcitabine 29–100%), so population-level inference is uncertain.",
    "AICAR-specific metabolism (e.g., adenosine kinase phosphorylation) is not covered by the supplied records."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted high (F >= 20%) because directly transferable nucleoside-analog contexts show measured F above 20%, ribavirin shows high solubility and substantial oral exposure, L5 supports transporter-mediated intestinal uptake of polar nucleosides, and L6 shows low hepatic metabolic clearance for nucleoside analogs (GS-441524 not CYP substrate; zidovudine low predicted hepatic clearance; EdUrd low hepatic clearance). No direct AICAR measurement is available, so confidence remains moderate."
}

